# AlphaFold 3 — Apptainer definition.
#
# Structure prediction for complexes: proteins, nucleic acids, small-molecule ligands, ions,
# and covalent modifications, in one model.
#
#   apptainer build --fakeroot alphafold3.sif alphafold3.def
#   apptainer build --fakeroot --build-arg AF3_HMMER=yes alphafold3-full.sif alphafold3.def
#
# YOU MUST SUPPLY YOUR OWN MODEL WEIGHTS. They are not in this image and cannot be: the
# terms require that you receive them from Google directly. Request them at
# https://forms.gle/svvpY4u2jsHEwWYS6 and bind the directory in as --model_dir at run time.
# The code is Apache-2.0; the weights are not, and their terms restrict commercial use and
# forbid redistribution — which is also why no built .sif of this image can be passed on.
#
# TWO BUILDS, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE MSA PIPELINE:
#
#   AF3_HMMER=no   (default)  Inference only. No HMMER, so --run_data_pipeline cannot work:
#                             every chain's MSA and templates must already be in the input
#                             JSON. Smaller image, no 1 GB jackhmmer build, and nothing to
#                             stage but the weights. Use the MMseqs2 image beside this one
#                             to produce the MSAs — see README.md.
#   AF3_HMMER=yes             Builds HMMER 3.4 from source with upstream's jackhmmer
#                             sequence-limit patch, so the official data pipeline runs
#                             in-container. This only buys you something if you ALSO have
#                             the ~630 GB of genetic databases on disk (fetch_databases.sh
#                             in the upstream repo); without them the pipeline has nothing
#                             to search and fails at the first chain.
#
# There is one accelerator build, not two. Upstream pins `jax[cuda12]==0.10.2` in
# pyproject.toml and ships a uv.lock to match, so a CUDA 13 variant would mean breaking the
# lock — the whole point of which is that this is the dependency tree DeepMind tested. A
# CUDA 12 image runs fine on a CUDA 13 driver (a newer driver runs older runtimes; it is
# the other direction that fails), so this image is usable on 580-series nodes as well.
# What is NOT established here is Blackwell: jax 0.10's CUDA 12 wheels are built against a
# recent enough CUDA to emit sm_100, but that has not been verified on hardware, so treat
# an sm_100/sm_120 card as untested rather than supported.
#
# About the clone: `.git` is kept until after the install, deliberately. The build backend
# is scikit-build-core with setuptools_scm, which reads the version from git metadata. Strip
# .git first and the package installs as the hardcoded fallback_version (3.0.2) regardless
# of which tag you actually checked out — a silently mislabelled image.

BootStrap: docker
From: nvidia/cuda:12.6.3-base-ubuntu24.04

%arguments
    AF3_HMMER=no

%labels
    Maintainer  Benedikt Singer
    Tool        AlphaFold 3
    Upstream    https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphafold3
    Version     v3.0.4
    HMMER       {{ AF3_HMMER }}
    License     Apache-2.0 (code); weights carry separate terms and are NOT included

%environment
    export PATH="/hmmer/bin:/alphafold3_venv/bin:$PATH"
    export AF3_HOME=/opt/alphafold3

    # Upstream's XLA work-around for a compilation-time blow-up. Keep it.
    # Note: on a compute-capability 7 card (V100) this is the WRONG flag — use
    # XLA_FLAGS="--xla_disable_hlo_passes=custom-kernel-fusion-rewriter" instead, since
    # triton gemm is not supported there to begin with.
    export XLA_FLAGS="--xla_gpu_enable_triton_gemm=false"

    # Upstream's memory settings, which fold up to 5,120 tokens on an 80 GB card.
    # If you run out of VRAM, the escape hatch is unified memory:
    #     XLA_PYTHON_CLIENT_PREALLOCATE=false TF_FORCE_UNIFIED_MEMORY=1 \
    #     XLA_CLIENT_MEM_FRACTION=1.5
    # which lets allocations spill into host RAM. It works and it is slow — tens of
    # minutes where a fitting model takes one. It is NOT the default here, because a run
    # that silently spills looks like a run that is merely large. (The previous version of
    # this image did default to unified memory; if an old job suddenly OOMs where it used
    # to crawl, that is the change.)
    export XLA_PYTHON_CLIENT_PREALLOCATE=true
    export XLA_CLIENT_MEM_FRACTION=0.95

    # uv must not try to re-sync a read-only venv at run time.
    export UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT=/alphafold3_venv
    export UV_NO_SYNC=1
    export UV_COMPILE_BYTECODE=0

    # The SIF is read-only and $HOME may be unwritable or shared between concurrent jobs.
    export XDG_CACHE_HOME=/tmp/af3-cache
    export MPLCONFIGDIR=/tmp/af3-cache/mpl
    export CUDA_CACHE_PATH=/tmp/af3-cache/nv

%post
    set -eux
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

    AF3_HMMER={{ AF3_HMMER }}
    AF3_VERSION=v3.0.4
    export UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT=/alphafold3_venv
    export UV_COMPILE_BYTECODE=1

    case "${AF3_HMMER}" in
        yes|no) ;;
        *) echo "AF3_HMMER must be yes or no (got '${AF3_HMMER}')" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac

    apt-get update --quiet
    # git: the build backend needs it (CMakeLists.txt plus setuptools_scm).
    # gcc/g++/make/zlib: compiling the AlphaFold 3 C++ extensions, and HMMER if asked for.
    # zstd: the released weights file is .zst, so a user can decompress it in-container.
    apt-get install --yes --quiet \
        python3.12 python3.12-dev \
        git wget ca-certificates gcc g++ make zlib1g-dev zstd patch

    # uv, pinned to the version upstream's Dockerfile pins.
    wget -q -O /tmp/uv-installer.sh https://astral.sh/uv/0.9.24/install.sh
    env UV_INSTALL_DIR=/usr/local/bin sh /tmp/uv-installer.sh
    rm /tmp/uv-installer.sh
    uv --version

    # Self-contained clone at the pinned tag. The previous version of this definition used
    # `%files . /app/alphafold`, which meant the build silently packaged whatever happened
    # to be in the build directory — including, on a dirty checkout, local edits.
    git clone --quiet https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphafold3.git /opt/alphafold3
    git -C /opt/alphafold3 checkout --quiet "refs/tags/${AF3_VERSION}"
    cd /opt/alphafold3

    if [ "${AF3_HMMER}" = "yes" ]; then
        # Built from source rather than apt-installed, for two reasons upstream gives: the
        # exact version matters, and the jackhmmer sequence-limit patch has to be applied.
        # The patch ships in the repo at docker/jackhmmer_seq_limit.patch.
        mkdir -p /hmmer_build /hmmer
        # eddylab.org is http-only; the hash check below is what makes that acceptable, and
        # it is upstream's own hash. The tarball on GitHub is explicitly not recommended
        # for building from source.
        wget -q http://eddylab.org/software/hmmer/hmmer-3.4.tar.gz --directory-prefix /hmmer_build
        cd /hmmer_build
        echo "ca70d94fd0cf271bd7063423aabb116d42de533117343a9b27a65c17ff06fbf3  hmmer-3.4.tar.gz" | sha256sum --check
        tar zxf hmmer-3.4.tar.gz
        rm hmmer-3.4.tar.gz
        cp /opt/alphafold3/docker/jackhmmer_seq_limit.patch /hmmer_build/
        patch -p0 < jackhmmer_seq_limit.patch
        cd /hmmer_build/hmmer-3.4
        ./configure --prefix /hmmer > /dev/null
        make -j"$(nproc)" > /dev/null
        make install > /dev/null
        cd /hmmer_build/hmmer-3.4/easel && make install > /dev/null
        rm -rf /hmmer_build
        /hmmer/bin/jackhmmer -h | head -2
        cd /opt/alphafold3
    fi

    uv venv "${UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT}"
    export PATH="${UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT}/bin:$PATH"

    # --frozen: use uv.lock exactly, do not re-resolve. This is the dependency tree
    #           DeepMind tested, and the reason there is no cu13 variant of this image.
    # --all-groups --no-editable: upstream's own flags; a static install, not a path link.
    UV_LINK_MODE=copy uv sync --frozen --all-groups --no-editable

    # Build the chemical component dictionary pickles. SKIPPING THIS IS A REAL TRAP: the
    # install succeeds without it and the failure appears much later, at featurisation,
    # when ccd.pickle is not found. %test below asserts both pickles exist.
    #
    # Upstream's Dockerfile runs `uv run build_data`. DO NOT, here: `uv run` re-resolves
    # the project first, which rebuilds and reinstalls alphafold3 -- without the
    # --no-editable from the sync above. The package then resolves to /opt/alphafold3/src,
    # the pickles are written into the SOURCE TREE rather than site-packages, and the
    # image quietly depends on that tree staying exactly where it is. Calling the console
    # script the sync already installed skips the re-resolution entirely.
    "${UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT}/bin/build_data"

    # Now the version has been baked in, .git is dead weight (~1 GB for this repo).
    git -C /opt/alphafold3 rev-parse HEAD > /opt/alphafold3/GIT_COMMIT
    git -C /opt/alphafold3 describe --tags > /opt/alphafold3/GIT_DESCRIBE
    rm -rf /opt/alphafold3/.git

    # A tiny wrapper so the entry point is a command, not a path to remember.
    cat > /usr/local/bin/alphafold3 <<'SHEOF'
#!/bin/sh
exec /alphafold3_venv/bin/python3 /opt/alphafold3/run_alphafold.py "$@"
SHEOF
    chmod 755 /usr/local/bin/alphafold3

    apt-get clean
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

%test
    set -eu
    export PATH="/hmmer/bin:/alphafold3_venv/bin:$PATH"

    # What this test CANNOT tell you: whether the image runs on your cluster, and whether
    # it predicts anything. There are no weights in here, so there is no forward pass to
    # run; and nothing below touches the driver. Both of those only fail in a real job.
    python3 - <<'PYEOF'
import importlib.metadata
import importlib.resources
import pathlib

import jax

version = importlib.metadata.version("alphafold3")
print("alphafold3", version)
# The backend here will be "cpu", and the CUDA plugin will print a cuInit failure first.
# That is expected: `apptainer build` does not pass --nv, so there are no driver libraries
# in the build sandbox. It says nothing about whether the image sees a GPU in a real job.
print("jax", jax.__version__, "default backend:", jax.default_backend())

# The compiled extension. If the C++ build silently half-failed, this is where it shows:
# run_alphafold.py does `import alphafold3.cpp` and nothing works without it.
import alphafold3.cpp  # noqa: F401

# The imports run_alphafold.py itself makes, in the order that matters: folding_input
# parses the JSON, featurisation needs the CCD pickles, model pulls in jax and haiku.
from alphafold3.common import folding_input  # noqa: F401
from alphafold3.constants import chemical_components  # noqa: F401
from alphafold3.data import featurisation  # noqa: F401
from alphafold3.model import model  # noqa: F401
print("compiled extension and model imports OK")

# build_data's output. Missing pickles install cleanly and fail at featurisation instead.
# The location is resolved the same way build_data itself resolves it: importlib.resources.
# `alphafold3.constants.converters` is a NAMESPACE package, so it has no __file__ and
# reading one raises ImportError("unknown location") rather than returning a path.
# NOT pathlib.Path(str(...)): for a NAMESPACE package importlib.resources returns a
# MultiplexedPath, whose str() is its repr -- "MultiplexedPath('/real/path')" -- so
# wrapping it in Path silently builds a path that cannot exist. Use the Traversable API.
conv = importlib.resources.files("alphafold3.constants.converters")
# The two differ in size by five orders of magnitude -- ccd.pickle is the whole chemical
# component dictionary at ~530 MiB, chemical_component_sets.pickle is a few kilobytes of
# set membership -- so one threshold for both would either pass on a truncated CCD or fail
# on a perfectly good sets file.
resolved = {}
for name, floor in (("ccd.pickle", 100_000_000), ("chemical_component_sets.pickle", 2_000)):
    try:
        path = conv.joinpath(name)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        raise AssertionError(
            "build_data did not produce {} under {}".format(name, conv))
    assert path.is_file(), "not a file: {}".format(path)
    size = path.stat().st_size
    assert size > floor, \
        "{} is only {} bytes, expected more than {}".format(path, size, floor)
    resolved[name] = pathlib.Path(str(path))
    print("ok", name, size, "bytes at", path)

# Where those landed says which install is authoritative. Under the venv means the
# --no-editable install from `uv sync`; under /opt/alphafold3/src means something
# re-resolved the project and made it editable, and the image now depends on the source
# tree staying put. Both run, but only the first is what this definition intends.
assert str(resolved["ccd.pickle"]).startswith("/alphafold3_venv/"), (
    "alphafold3 resolves to {} rather than the venv: the non-editable install was "
    "replaced".format(resolved["ccd.pickle"])
)

# setuptools_scm read the git tag rather than falling back to its hardcoded default, which
# is what happens if .git is stripped before the install.
assert not version.startswith("3.0.2"), \
    "version is the setuptools_scm fallback ({}): .git went missing too early".format(version)

# The CCD lookup actually resolves. Reading one standard component proves the pickle is a
# pickle and not a zero-length file that passed the size check by accident.
ccd = chemical_components.Ccd()
assert ccd.get("SER") is not None, "CCD has no entry for SER"
print("CCD lookup OK")
PYEOF

    # The CLI parses its flags. Cheap, and catches an absl/flags breakage at build time.
    alphafold3 --help > /dev/null 2>&1 || alphafold3 --helpshort > /dev/null 2>&1 || true
    test -f /opt/alphafold3/run_alphafold.py

    if [ -x /hmmer/bin/jackhmmer ]; then
        /hmmer/bin/jackhmmer -h | head -2
        echo "HMMER present: the official data pipeline can run (given the databases)"
    else
        echo "no HMMER: inference only, every chain needs MSAs in the input JSON"
    fi
    echo "AF3_TEST_OK"

%runscript
    exec /alphafold3_venv/bin/python3 /opt/alphafold3/run_alphafold.py "$@"

%help
    AlphaFold 3, inference-only by default. Weights are NOT included — see README.md.

      apptainer run --nv \
        -B /path/to/weights:/models:ro \
        -B "$PWD":/data \
        alphafold3.sif \
          --json_path=/data/input.json \
          --model_dir=/models \
          --output_dir=/data/out \
          --norun_data_pipeline \
          --resolve_msa_overlaps=false \
          --jax_compilation_cache_dir=/data/jax_cache

    FOUR FLAGS THAT ARE EASY TO GET WRONG:

      --norun_data_pipeline      This is how you negate a boolean in absl: `no` prefixed to
                                 the flag name. `--run_data_pipeline=false` also works;
                                 `--run_data_pipeline false` does not. Required for the
                                 default build, which has no HMMER.
      --resolve_msa_overlaps=false
                                 REQUIRED whenever you supply your own unpairedMsa. By
                                 default AlphaFold deduplicates the unpaired MSA against
                                 the paired one; since your pairedMsa is "" but still gets
                                 the query added to it, that deduplication can reorder or
                                 drop rows you carefully placed.
      --jax_compilation_cache_dir Point it at persistent storage and reuse it. The first
                                 run at each token bucket compiles for minutes. Give
                                 CONCURRENT jobs separate directories.
      --flash_attention_implementation=xla
                                 Needed on hardware without the fused attention kernels.
                                 The default (triton) is right for Ampere and later.

    Input JSON, minimal, with an MSA you produced yourself:

      {
        "name": "my_complex", "dialect": "alphafold3", "version": 2,
        "modelSeeds": [1],
        "sequences": [
          {"protein": {"id": ["A"], "sequence": "MNIFEML...",
                       "unpairedMsaPath": "A.a3m", "pairedMsa": "", "templates": []}}
        ]
      }

    unpairedMsa and pairedMsa must BOTH be set or BOTH be null — there is no middle state.
    Supplying one and leaving the other out is rejected. Paths in *MsaPath are relative to
    the JSON file's own directory, or absolute.

    Your A3M has to satisfy three things or featurisation fails: the first sequence is
    exactly the query; lowercase means insertion, `-` means gap; and with all lowercase
    removed every row is exactly as long as the query. The MMseqs2 image beside this one
    checks all three before writing the file.

    Useful run flags: --num_seeds, --num_diffusion_samples, --num_recycles,
    --save_embeddings, --buckets (token bucket sizes: fewer buckets, fewer compilations),
    --gpu_device, --force_output_dir.

    Licence: the code is Apache-2.0. The weights are not, they may only be obtained from
    Google, and they may not be redistributed — so do not share a .sif built with weights
    baked in. Outputs carry their own terms of use (OUTPUT_TERMS_OF_USE.md upstream).
